				"content": "What is a goroutine?"
			},
			{
				"role": "assistant",
				"content": "A goroutine is a lightweight thread of execution managed by the Go runtime rather than the operating system. You start one by placing the `go` keyword before a function call, such as `go doSomething()`, and it runs concurrently with the rest of your program. Goroutines are cheap to create (they start with a small, growable stack), so a Go program can run thousands or even millions of them at once."
			},
			{
				"role": "user",
				"content": "And how do I stop one?"
			},
			{
				"role": "assistant",
				"content": "There's no direct way to kill a goroutine from outside it. The idiomatic approach is to signal it to stop and let it exit on its own, usually by passing a `context.Context` and checking `ctx.Done()` in a `select` statement, or by listening on a done channel. For example:\n\n```go\nctx, cancel := context.WithCancel(context.Background())\ngo func() {\n    for {\n        select {\n        case <-ctx.Done():\n            return\n        default:\n            // do work\n        }\n    }\n}()\ncancel() // signals the goroutine to stop\n```\n\nCalling `cancel()` closes the context's Done channel, and the goroutine returns the next time it checks. If the goroutine is blocked on something else, include `ctx.Done()` in that `select` as well so it can wake up."
			},
			{
				"role": "user",
				"content": "Show me a two-line example."
			}
		]
